A global struct initialiser is as wide as the struct.

WHAT IT IS. The initialiser emitted the members back to back and stopped.
Nothing put the PADDING in -- neither the gaps between members nor the tail
that takes the struct up to its alignment -- so the bytes written were the sum
of the member sizes while every accessor strides by the struct's SIZE.

For one struct that is invisible: the members are at their right offsets and
nothing reads past the last one. For an ARRAY of them the two numbers disagree
by the padding on every element and the drift compounds.

    struct TCCOption { const char *name; uint16_t index, flags; };

    emitted    &str %0 $1 $0        8 + 2 + 2 = 12 bytes per entry
    accessed   stride 16, index at 8, flags at 10

tcc's option table is exactly that shape.

WHAT IT COST. A single `-B` flag segfaulted a one-line program.

    /tmp/triv.c                          exit 1
    -B. /tmp/triv.c                      exit 139, SIGNAL 11
    -I. /tmp/triv.c                      exit 1
    -Iinclude /tmp/triv.c                exit 1

tcc_parse_args walks tcc_options with `++popt`. Entry 0 read correctly; entry 1
was read four bytes early, so its `name` came back as 0x100000000 -- the tail of
entry 0 spliced to the head of entry 1 -- and was dereferenced.

    A1-scan-start table=5636740
    A2-popt=5636740   A3-name=5717135      valid, points at "h"
    A2-popt=5636756   A3-name=4294967296   0x100000000

AND IT MEANS A CI STEP HAS BEEN MEASURING THE WRONG THING FOR ROUNDS. The
"tcctest.c" step passes `-B. -I. -Iinclude`. It was never reaching tcctest.c:
it died in option parsing before the file was opened. Every reading of that
step as "micro-c's tcc cannot compile tcc's test file" was unfounded. With this
patch both inputs behave identically, which is the tell that the input was
never the variable:

    -B. /tmp/triv.c                              exit 1, no signal
    -B. -I. -Iinclude tests/tcctest.c            exit 1, no signal

THE FIX pads to each member's own `offset` rather than assuming the members are
contiguous, then pads the tail to type_size->size. The offset form is not
belt-and-braces: `struct { char c; int i; }` has a seven-byte gap before `i`,
and that gap is the same bug in miniature -- it would have been left out too.

WHAT THIS DOES NOT FIX. crt1.o and crti.o are still not found, so tcc_add_file
declines and everything stops at the driver's D4 marker with exit 1. That is
the next thing to look at and it may be harness configuration rather than a
compiler bug; no claim either way is made here.

A NOTE ON THE PREVIOUS PATCH. EXPERIMENT-zzh said "the P151 fault is closed"
and the CI gate written with it failed on its first run. Both were right about
the bug and wrong about the scope: zzh fixed a real corruption -- every symbol
tcc created had its token wiped -- but it was measured on a one-line file and
generalised. The gate has since been moved to the trivial-input step, where the
before/after was actually measured. This patch is what closed the remaining
signal, and it is being described in terms of the two inputs it was measured
on, not in terms of "the fault".

MEASURED

    -B. on a one-line file    SIGNAL 11 -> exit 1
    tcctest.c with flags      SIGNAL 11 -> exit 1
    difftest                  61 pass / 0 fail / 1 known gap, both architectures
    case 63                   the construct in 20 lines, red before, green after
    vocabulary                clean on five architectures
    corpus                    423 of 426 on aarch64

diff --git a/cc_core.c b/cc_core.c
index c41f77a..afcbe42 100644
--- a/cc_core.c
+++ b/cc_core.c
@@ -5615,6 +5615,30 @@ void global_struct_initializer_list(struct type* type_size)
 
 	struct type* member = type_size->members;
 
+	/* A GLOBAL STRUCT INITIALISER MUST BE AS WIDE AS THE STRUCT.
+	 *
+	 * This emitted the members back to back and stopped. Nothing put the
+	 * PADDING in -- neither the gaps between members nor the tail that takes
+	 * the struct up to its alignment -- so the bytes written were the sum of
+	 * the member sizes while every access used the struct's SIZE as its
+	 * stride. For one struct that is invisible. For an ARRAY of them the two
+	 * disagree by the padding on every element and the drift compounds:
+	 *
+	 *     struct TCCOption { const char *name; uint16_t index, flags; };
+	 *
+	 *     emitted   &str %0 $1 $0        8 + 2 + 2 = 12 bytes
+	 *     accessed  stride 16, index at 8, flags at 10
+	 *
+	 * tcc's option table is exactly that. `-B.` made tcc_parse_args walk to
+	 * the second entry, read its `name` four bytes early -- 0x100000000, the
+	 * tail of entry 0 spliced to the head of entry 1 -- and dereference it.
+	 * A single `-B` flag segfaulted a one-line program.
+	 *
+	 * The cursor pads to each member's own offset rather than assuming the
+	 * members are contiguous, because `struct { char c; int i; }` has a gap
+	 * before `i` and that gap is the same bug in miniature. */
+	int cursor = 0;
+
 	do
 	{
 		if(member == NULL)
@@ -5624,7 +5648,14 @@ void global_struct_initializer_list(struct type* type_size)
 			exit(EXIT_FAILURE);
 		}
 
+		while(cursor < member->offset)
+		{
+			global_value_output(0, 1);
+			cursor = cursor + 1;
+		}
+
 		global_value_selection(member->type);
+		cursor = member->offset + member->size;
 
 		member = member->members;
 
@@ -5637,10 +5668,23 @@ void global_struct_initializer_list(struct type* type_size)
 
 	while(member != NULL)
 	{
+		while(cursor < member->offset)
+		{
+			global_value_output(0, 1);
+			cursor = cursor + 1;
+		}
 		global_value_output(0, member->size);
+		cursor = member->offset + member->size;
 		member = member->members;
 	}
 
+	/* THE TAIL. sizeof is what the accessor strides by. */
+	while(cursor < type_size->size)
+	{
+		global_value_output(0, 1);
+		cursor = cursor + 1;
+	}
+
 	globals_list = emit("\n", globals_list);
 
 	require_match("Struct assignment initialization is invalid for globals.", "}");
